WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 8.6 · Marketing & Communications
Social media

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
When you are starting out, one social channel done well beats five done badly.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:28.133
Social media lets you reach your priority audiences where they already spend time. Choose the channel your audience uses, not the one you like best, give it a clear purpose and set rules from day one.

4
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:29.133
Choosing and running a channel

5
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:40.463
Fit the audience: Go where priority audiences already are, thinking about language and data costs. For example: A broadcast list for Saturday regulars, in the language they use at the market.

6
00:00:40.463 --> 00:00:48.459
Give it a purpose: Community, news, recruiting, support, advocacy or customer service. For example: The bakery’s social account exists to find café buyers.

7
00:00:48.459 --> 00:00:58.307
Mix the content: Stories, practical information, behind the scenes, calls to action and replies. For example: Monday: opening times; Wednesday: a trainee at work; Friday: order now.

8
00:00:58.307 --> 00:01:10.837
Rules from day one: Who posts, what needs approval, how you moderate, and shared access to logins. For example: Account opened with the bakery’s email; a co-founder can also reach the password.

9
00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:11.837
Why it matters

10
00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:16.400
1. One channel done well beats five done badly.

11
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.333
2. Safety rules protect participants, their images and their data.

12
00:01:21.333 --> 00:01:28.007
3. Shared logins mean you never lose an account with one person.

13
00:01:28.407 --> 00:01:29.007
What it connects to

14
00:01:29.007 --> 00:01:33.200
1. Your content calendar, which fills each channel.

15
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.133
2. Your campaigns, which use it to drive one action.

16
00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:44.067
3. Your social media policy, which grows from these rules.

17
00:01:44.467 --> 00:01:45.067
What to avoid

18
00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:49.259
Opening accounts on every platform at once.

19
00:01:49.259 --> 00:01:54.563
A personal email, or one person holding all the logins.

20
00:01:54.563 --> 00:01:59.385
Posting images of participants without consent.

21
00:01:59.985 --> 00:02:01.785
You choose your first channels and how each is managed.

22
00:02:01.785 --> 00:02:05.785
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:07.285 --> 00:02:10.385
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
